---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import AffiliateCta from '../components/AffiliateCta.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import RelatedLinks from '../components/RelatedLinks.astro';

const steps = [
  {
    name: 'Write a logline',
    text: 'One or two sentences. Who it is, what they want, and the tone. That sentence is the brief, not the finished shot list.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Choose the cast',
    text: 'Pick actors from the Casting Vault, or describe them. Character Lock saves a reference sheet so the face can travel from scene to scene.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Generate the storyboard',
    text: 'The Director Canvas breaks the logline into shots. You approve each still, frame by frame, before anything moves.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Animate and export',
    text: 'Animate the frames you approved, then stitch them into one film and download it without a Filmoyo watermark.',
  },
];

const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'Do I need a film-school script?',
    answer: 'No. A logline of one or two sentences is the start. You still read the frames and reject the ones that are wrong.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Why is this less work than prompting every shot?',
    answer:
      'Manual prompting means a new prompt, and often a new face, for every cut. The Director Canvas drafts the storyboard from the logline. You spend the time approving pictures, not inventing the whole film one isolated prompt at a time.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Is the export a finished Hollywood short?',
    answer: 'It is a stylized AI film. Length depends on the shots you approve and the credits you spend. It is not a live-action crew.',
  },
];

const faqSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faqs.map((faq) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: faq.question,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: faq.answer },
  })),
};

const howToSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'HowTo',
  name: 'How to create an AI short film',
  description: 'Go from a one or two sentence logline to an exported Filmoyo short by casting, approving frames, then animating.',
  step: steps.map((step, index) => ({
    '@type': 'HowToStep',
    position: index + 1,
    name: step.name,
    text: step.text,
  })),
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How to Create an AI Short Film (Step-by-Step Guide)"
  description="How to create an AI short film: write a logline, cast from the vault, approve the storyboard frame by frame, then animate and export in Filmoyo."
  canonicalURL="/how-to-create-ai-short-film"
  schema={[faqSchema, howToSchema]}
>
  <div class="container">
    <Breadcrumbs
      items={[
        { label: 'Home', href: '/' },
        { label: 'Create an AI short', href: '/how-to-create-ai-short-film' },
      ]}
    />
  </div>

  <section class="page-intro">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">Step by step</p>
      <h1>How to Create an AI Short Film from Scratch</h1>
      <p class="lede" style="margin-top: 0.9rem; max-width: 42rem;">
        Start with a blank page. End with one cinematic video you can download. The path is a logline, a cast, frames you accept, then the animation.
      </p>
      <div class="hero-actions" style="margin-top: 1.3rem; justify-content: flex-start;">
        <AffiliateCta id="get-filmoyo" extraClass="btn-lg">Get Filmoyo Director →</AffiliateCta>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container stack-lg">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">The steps</p>
        <h2 class="section-title">Four moves from a sentence to a file.</h2>
      </div>
      <ol class="claim-steps">
        {steps.map((step) => (
          <li>
            <strong>{step.name}</strong>
            <span>{step.text}</span>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>
      <p class="lede">
        The Director Canvas is what makes the middle of that list lighter. It drafts the shots from the logline, so you are not writing a fresh prompt for every cut. You still approve the pictures. That is the work. It is not a measured “ten times faster.” It is the difference between directing a draft and prompting in the dark.
      </p>
      <div class="hero-actions" style="justify-content: flex-start;">
        <AffiliateCta extraClass="btn-lg">Draft the storyboard →</AffiliateCta>
      </div>

      <div class="split">
        <figure class="screen">
          <img class="frame-top" src="/images/logline.jpg" width="1600" height="900" alt="Filmoyo Direct tab with a shot prompt and the Wayfinder selected" loading="lazy" />
          <figcaption>The shot, after the logline has been broken down.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="screen">
          <img src="/images/storyboard.jpg" width="1600" height="900" alt="A Filmoyo frame marked FRAME READY above a scene timeline" loading="lazy" />
          <figcaption>Approve the still. Then let it move.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
      <div class="hero-actions" style="justify-content: flex-start;">
        <AffiliateCta>Start the short →</AffiliateCta>
      </div>

      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">FAQ</p>
        <h2 class="section-title">What you still have to do.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-list">
        {faqs.map((faq) => (
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary>{faq.question}</summary>
            <p>{faq.answer}</p>
          </details>
        ))}
      </div>
      <div class="hero-actions" style="justify-content: flex-start;">
        <AffiliateCta extraClass="btn-lg">Get Filmoyo Director →</AffiliateCta>
      </div>
      <RelatedLinks extra={[{ href: '/filmoyo-character-lock-explained', label: 'Character Lock explained' }]} />
      <div class="final-band">
        <p class="eyebrow">Then export</p>
        <h2 class="section-title">A short is the shots you were willing to approve.</h2>
        <p class="lede narrow" style="margin-top: 0.8rem;">
          The <a class="text-link" href="/">review</a> shows the demo. <a class="text-link" href="/filmoyo-pricing">Pricing</a> is $27 or $37. The <a class="text-link" href="/filmoyo-bonus">logline bonus</a> is a pile of starters if the blank page is the hard part.
        </p>
        <div class="hero-actions" style="margin-top: 1.1rem;">
          <AffiliateCta extraClass="btn-lg">Get Filmoyo Director →</AffiliateCta>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
